---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { tree } from '../../lib/tree';
const c = tree.counts;
---

<Base title="The seven branches · The Upgrade Tree" description="The seven things about a person that technology can change, and what has been recorded under each.">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">The branches</p>
    <h1>Seven things about a person that can be changed</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every upgrade on the tree sits under one of these. The count on the home page adds them all up;
      this page shows them one branch at a time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <ul class="branches">
      {tree.branches.map((b) => (
        <li>
          <a class="bcard" href={b.path}>
            <div class="bcard-top"><span class="n">{b.order} of 7</span><span>{b.count === 0 ? 'nothing yet' : `${b.count} upgrade${b.count === 1 ? '' : 's'}`}</span></div>
            <div class="bcard-title">{b.name}</div>
            <div class="bcard-sub">{b.tagline}</div>
            <div class="bcard-foot">{b.furthest_label ? `Furthest stage: ${b.furthest_label.toLowerCase()}` : 'No stage reached'}</div>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose">
    <h2>The stages</h2>
    <p>
      Each upgrade sits on one rung of a five-rung ladder. It moves up when a public record shows the
      next thing has happened, and it never moves down: if a device is later withdrawn, the record
      says so, but the rung it reached stays.
    </p>
    <ol>
      {tree.stages.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.label}.</strong> {s.meaning}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <p class="dim">
      {c.upgrades} upgrade{c.upgrades === 1 ? '' : 's'} on the tree, {c.first_human_or_beyond} at a first human or beyond.
      How a stage is decided: <a href="/about/#method">how the numbers are worked out</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
